---
import GuidePage from '../../components/GuidePage.astro';
---

<GuidePage
  title="Quick Start: Plan One Quilt Project"
  description="Use one small worked example to learn QuiltClarity from a blank project through Buy now and the cutting plan."
  eyebrow="Start Here · Step 1 of 2"
  guideSlug="getting-started"
  guideCategory="start_here"
  next={{
    href: '/guides/project-planner-tutorial/',
    label: 'Full Project Planner Tutorial',
  }}
>
  <aside class="card callout">
    <h2>What QuiltClarity does</h2>
    <p>
      It reconciles rectangular cuts from an existing pattern or plan with
      measured fabric you already own, then plans only the additional purchase
      and shows how every piece is cut.
    </p>
    <p>
      It does not read pattern files, design the quilt, or maintain a permanent
      stash.
    </p>
  </aside>

  <section id="before-you-start">
    <h2>Before you start</h2>
    <p>
      Have the cut list and the measured width and length of any fabric pieces
      you want to use. For this example, use inches and the default ¼-inch seam
      allowance.
    </p>
    <div class="example-data">
      <h3>Worked example</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Fabric: <strong>Blue</strong>, 40-inch usable WOF</li>
        <li>Stock: one custom piece, <strong>20 × 10 inches</strong></li>
        <li>Cuts: <strong>four 10 × 10-inch cut squares</strong></li>
        <li>Pattern says: <strong>½ yard</strong> (optional)</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>1. Open the planner and name the project</h2>
    <p>
      Open the planner in the button below. Enter <strong>Quick Start</strong> as
      the optional project name and leave Measurements set to Inches and yards.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>2. Configure the fabric</h2>
    <p>
      Rename the first fabric <strong>Blue</strong>. Leave usable width at 40
      inches. Usable WOF is the width you can actually cut after removing the
      selvages.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>3. Add the fabric you already have</h2>
    <p>
      Choose <strong>Add custom piece</strong>. Enter 20 inches across the
      fabric, 10 inches lengthwise, and quantity 1. Each stock entry is a real
      physical rectangle; separate remnants never become one imaginary joined
      piece.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>4. Enter the cuts</h2>
    <p>
      In the first cut row choose Blue, label it <strong>Square</strong>, enter
      quantity 4, width 10, height 10, and choose <strong>Cut</strong>. Cut
      means the dimensions already include seam allowance.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>5. Optionally enter the pattern amount</h2>
    <p>
      Open <strong>Compare with pattern yardage</strong> and enter 0.5 yard for Pattern
      says. This is only a reference; it will not be reduced by stock.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>6. Calculate the project plan</h2>
    <p>
      Select <strong>Calculate Cutting Plan</strong>. The 20 × 10-inch stock
      piece holds two squares. The remaining two squares fit across a new
      40-inch-wide bolt in 10 inches of raw length. With the default safety and
      ⅛-yard purchase increment, <strong>Buy now is ⅜ yard</strong>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>7. Read the three different amounts</h2>
    <dl class="guide-definition-list">
      <div><dt>Pattern says</dt><dd>The external ½-yard reference.</dd></div>
      <div>
        <dt>Fresh-fabric plan</dt><dd>
          What all four squares require if purchased new, ignoring stock.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Buy now</dt><dd>
          The shortfall after the measured stock covers two squares.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>8. Find the cutting plan</h2>
    <p>
      Under Blue, inspect <strong>Existing-stock allocations</strong> and <strong
        >Purchased fabric</strong
      >. Each section has cutting instructions, a diagram built from the same
      placements, and a text version.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>You completed the Quick Start</h2>
    <p>
      You created a project, entered stock and cuts, calculated the shortfall,
      distinguished the main result concepts, and located the cutting plan.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a
        class="button"
        href="/fabric-cutting-planner/"
        data-guide-action="complete">Open Project Planner</a
      >
      <a href="/guides/project-planner-tutorial/"
        >Next: Full Project Planner Tutorial</a
      >
    </div>
  </section>
</GuidePage>
